course: dictionary
lesson: 14427
title: Words that make things bigger
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Overstated is an adjective, and it is also the past form of the verb overstate. It means said to be bigger, more important or worse than it really is. Another word for it is exaggerated. «The advert overstated how fast the toy car could go.» The advert made the car sound faster than it really was.
explain: To overstate something is to say it in a way that makes it seem bigger than it truly is. «The newspaper overstated the danger of the storm.» Read the words around overstated: the storm was not as dangerous as the newspaper said. Ask yourself, was the thing made to sound bigger or worse than it really was? If yes, the word is probably overstated.
explain: Writers use this idea too. «The importance of sleep cannot be overstated.» Here cannot be overstated means sleep is extremely important, so important that you could not make it sound too big. In an argument, an overstated claim is a weak claim, because readers stop trusting it.
fact: Overstated means said to be bigger, more important or worse than it really is.
fact: Exaggerated is a synonym for overstated, so the two words mean nearly the same.
fact: «Cannot be overstated» means something is extremely important.
fact: In persuasive writing, overstated claims can make an argument weaker, because readers may stop believing it.
fact: Overstated is also the past tense of the verb overstate: today I overstate, yesterday I overstated.
exercise: «The advert overstated how fast the toy car could go.» What does overstated mean? | *Made it sound bigger or better than it really was | Drew a picture of the toy car | Hid the toy car under a blanket | Made it sound quieter than it was
exercise: «The importance of sleep cannot be overstated.» What does this sentence tell us? | *Sleep is extremely important | Sleep is only for babies | Sleep is not important at all | Sleep is very short
exercise: Which word means about the same as overstated? | honest | *exaggerated | shy | calm
test: «Sam overstated the size of the fish he caught.» What is Sam doing? | Putting the fish back in the water | *Making the fish sound bigger than it was | Counting the fish carefully | Hiding the fish from his friends
test: In persuasive writing, what usually happens when a writer overstates a claim? | It makes the argument funnier | *It makes the argument weaker, because readers may stop trusting it | It makes the argument shorter | It makes the argument weaker
exam: «The news said the storm would be the worst ever, but the danger was overstated.» What does this sentence suggest? | *The storm was not as bad as the news said | The news was right about every detail | The storm was the worst ever recorded | The storm never happened at all
